{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536749774,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzNTk0MjY2MzE=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814","origin":"github"},"title":"question: organizational standards for research data?:","message":"hey Datalad (hey @yarikoptic !) \n\nWe are putting together an organizational and data movement strategy for research computing and the libraries at Stanford, and are looking at standards and tooling to help with versioning, organization and movement. See [this description](https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/2059) to get an overview of an \"ideal flow.\" I am thinking that DataLad could be a really good solution to handle the versioning and general interaction of a dataset! I'm wondering, how is the community creating definitions for organization, and then having datalad be able to plug into them? (You can talk about BIDS as an example!) We would want to be able to have a set of organizational templates that define formats and file structure/organization, and then plug that into use via datalad across Google Cloud storage, compute locations, and then archive. Your wisdom / thoughts / all of the above would be greatly appreciated - very excited to chat about this!\n\n@cmh2166 @hannahfrost @rmarinshaw","files":null}]}